## 6. Detailed migration nuances

### 6.1 Decorators → function handlers (the core change)

Delete `routing-controllers` controllers. Each controller class becomes a **Fastify plugin** exporting route registrations. Map the param decorators to direct request access:

| routing-controllers | Fastify |
|---|---|
| `@JsonController('/users')` + method decorators | a plugin that calls `fastify.get/post/...('/users/...', opts, handler)` |
| `@Get('/:id')` / `@Post()` / `@Put()` / `@Delete()` | `fastify.get('/:id', ...)` etc. |
| `@ReqPathParam('id')` / `@Param('id')` | `request.params.id` |
| `@ReqQueryParam('q')` / `@QueryParam('q')` | `request.query.q` |
| `@ReqHeaderParam('h')` / `@HeaderParam('h')` | `request.headers['h']` (lower-cased by Node) |
| `@Body()` | `request.body` |
| `@QueryParams()` / `@Params()` | the whole `request.query` / `request.params` object |
| `@Req()` / `@Res()` | `request` / `reply` |
| `@CurrentUser()` | `request.user`, populated by an auth `preHandler` hook (§6.4) |
| `@UploadedFile()` / `@UploadedFiles()` | `@fastify/multipart` |
| `@HttpCode(201)` | `reply.code(201)` or `response` schema keyed by status |
| return value (auto-serialized) | `return value` from the async handler (Fastify serializes via the response schema) |
| throwing `NotFoundError`, `BadRequestError`, … | throw `httpErrors.notFound()` / set `reply.code()` + throw (§6.8) |

**Before/after shape:**
```ts
// BEFORE
@JsonController('/users')
export class UserController {
  constructor(private svc: UserService) {}        // typedi injection
  @Get('/:id')
  @HttpCode(200)
  async getUser(@ReqPathParam('id') id: string,
                @ReqQueryParam('full') full: boolean) {
    const u = await this.svc.find(id, full);
    if (!u) throw new NotFoundError('user not found');
    return u;
  }
}

// AFTER — plugin, function handler, schema-driven
import type { FastifyPluginAsync } from 'fastify';
import { Type } from '@sinclair/typebox';

export const userRoutes: FastifyPluginAsync = async (fastify) => {
  const svc = makeUserService(/* deps */);          // see §6.4 on DI

  fastify.get('/users/:id', {
    schema: {
      params: Type.Object({ id: Type.String() }),
      querystring: Type.Object({ full: Type.Optional(Type.Boolean()) }),
      response: { 200: UserSchema, 404: ErrorSchema }, // 200 schema => compiled serializer
    },
  }, async (request, reply) => {
    const { id } = request.params;
    const { full } = request.query;
    const u = await svc.find(id, full ?? false);
    if (!u) throw fastify.httpErrors.notFound('user not found');
    return u;
  });
};
```
Note: validation/coercion now happens **before** the handler via the schema (e.g. `full` is coerced to boolean). Remove redundant manual parsing/validation from the handler body, but **only** once the schema reproduces the same constraints.

### 6.2 Validation: `class-validator` → JSON Schema / TypeBox

Each `class-validator` DTO becomes a schema. Preserve the **exact** constraints — do not loosen them. Reference mapping:

| class-validator | schema |
|---|---|
| `@IsString()` | `type: 'string'` |
| `@IsInt()` / `@IsNumber()` | `type: 'integer'` / `type: 'number'` |
| `@IsBoolean()` | `type: 'boolean'` (with coercion for querystring) |
| `@IsEmail()` | `format: 'email'` |
| `@Min(n)` / `@Max(n)` | `minimum` / `maximum` |
| `@Length(a,b)` / `@MinLength` / `@MaxLength` | `minLength` / `maxLength` |
| `@IsEnum(E)` | `enum: [...]` |
| `@IsOptional()` | omit from `required` |
| `@ValidateNested()` + `@Type(() => X)` | nested object schema |
| `@IsArray()` + element decorators | `type: 'array', items: {...}` |
| custom validators (`FutureDate`, `PastDate`, `BetweenDates`, `ValidDate`) | **custom AJV keywords/formats** or TypeBox refinements; see below |

**Custom validators** (`FutureDate`, etc.): register them as AJV custom keywords/formats on the Fastify AJV instance, or implement as a `preValidation`/post-parse check that throws the same error as today. Do **not** drop the semantics. If a custom validator encoded business rules (e.g. "date must be after today"), reproduce it precisely and unit-test it against the old behavior.

Set AJV options to match Express behavior where it matters (`coerceTypes`, `removeAdditional`, `useDefaults`). Decide explicitly whether unknown properties were previously allowed; mirror that with `additionalProperties`.

### 6.3 Middleware classes → lifecycle hooks (order matters)

Express runs middleware as `(req,res,next)` in registration order. Fastify has a **typed lifecycle**: `onRequest → preParsing → preValidation → preHandler → handler → preSerialization → onSend → onResponse`. Map each middleware to the **correct stage**, not blindly to `onRequest`:

| Middleware (current) | Fastify hook | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `ContextMiddleware` (request context / correlation) | `onRequest` + `AsyncLocalStorage` | establish the request store early; see §6.4 |
| auth / token validation | `preHandler` (or a route-scoped decorator) | sets `request.user` |
| request/response logging middleware | mostly **removed** → Pino `onResponse` | Pino logs req/res automatically; keep only custom fields |
| `TimeoutMiddleware` | route `connectionTimeout` / `preHandler` w/ `AbortController` | per-route timeouts are cleaner here |
| `NoCacheMiddleware` | `onSend` (set `Cache-Control`) or `@fastify/helmet` | header mutation belongs in `onSend` |
| body/raw capture | `preParsing` / content-type parser | §6.11 |

Scope hooks to the plugin/route where they applied, instead of globally, to preserve "this middleware only ran for these routes" semantics. Verify the **effective order** matches the old Express chain for any route where ordering had observable effects (e.g. auth before rate-limit vs after).

### 6.4 DI removal (`typedi`) + request context

`routing-controllers` resolved controllers and `@CurrentUser`/services through `typedi`. Going function-based, choose per service lifetime:

- **Singletons** (`@Service()` with no request state) → plain module-level singletons or factory functions. Simplest; prefer this.
- **Request-scoped state** (correlation IDs, per-request user/tenant, anything `ContextMiddleware` stored on the request) → **`AsyncLocalStorage`**. Establish the store in an `onRequest` hook and read it anywhere downstream. This replaces typedi's request container and is the idiomatic Fastify/Node approach.
- You **may** keep `typedi` purely as a DI container decoupled from routing if the service graph is large — but if you do, document why, and ensure it doesn't drag `reflect-metadata` decorator usage back into the routing layer.

Whichever you choose, **preserve service lifetimes** — turning a request-scoped service into a singleton (or vice-versa) is a behavior change and a potential data-leak-across-requests bug. Flag any service whose scope is ambiguous.

### 6.5 (reserved — see dependency table in §5)

### 6.6 GraphQL: Apollo Server 4 → `@as-integrations/fastify`

- Install `@as-integrations/fastify`. Construct `ApolloServer` and **include `fastifyApolloDrainPlugin(fastify)` in its `plugins`** array (required for graceful shutdown / draining).
- `await apollo.start()` **before** registering the integration.
- Apollo 4 moved context out of the constructor: provide a context function `(request, reply) => Promise<Context>` (typed `FastifyRequest`/`FastifyReply`). Port whatever the old context builder did (auth, loaders, tenant) into this function.
- Register either as a plugin (`fastify.register(fastifyApollo(apollo), { context })`) on the existing GraphQL path, or via `fastifyApolloHandler` inside an explicit route. Keep the **same path and methods** as today.
- **Subscriptions:** Apollo Server 4 does **not** handle WS subscriptions itself. If the current setup has GraphQL subscriptions, that is a separate `graphql-ws` + `@fastify/websocket` wiring — **do not assume it carries over; flag it** (§9).

### 6.7 WebSockets (`ws` + decorators) and SSE

- **WebSockets:** `@fastify/websocket` is built on `ws`. Replace `WebSocketDecorators` with `fastify.get('/path', { websocket: true }, (connection, request) => { ... })`. Note the connection/lifecycle API differs from raw `ws`; port handlers accordingly and preserve auth (do it in `preValidation`/`onRequest` for the upgrade route).
- **SSE (`SSE.ts`) is not WebSockets.** It's a long-lived `text/event-stream` HTTP response. Implement as a route that writes to `reply.raw` (or use a small SSE plugin), sets the right headers, and handles client disconnect. Keep the event format and heartbeat behavior identical. Migrate SSE and WS as **separate** concerns.

### 6.8 Error handling + error-shape contract

- `routing-controllers` had `HttpError` subclasses and a global error handler producing a specific JSON error shape. **Capture that exact shape first** (status code + body) from the current code/tests.
- In Fastify, set a global `setErrorHandler` (and optionally use `@fastify/sensible`'s `httpErrors`) that reproduces the same body for the same conditions. Map each old error class → equivalent thrown error.
- **Validation errors:** Fastify's default 400 body differs from `class-validator`'s. If clients depend on the old validation-error shape, override it via `setErrorHandler` and/or `schemaErrorFormatter` to match. This is a contract item, not cosmetic.
- Ensure thrown errors still get traced/reported to APM (§6.10).

### 6.9 Logging: drop `morgan` → built-in Pino

- Configure Fastify's logger (Pino). Set `genReqId` to derive from the incoming correlation header if one exists, and wire `request.id` to the OTel trace ID so logs ↔ traces correlate.
- **Banking/PII redaction is mandatory.** Use Pino's `redact` to strip auth headers, tokens, account numbers, PANs, and any sensitive body fields from logs. Inventory what the current logging exposed and ensure the new logs are at least as redacted (preferably more).
- Reproduce any log fields downstream systems parse (request id, latency, status, route). Remove `morgan` only after parity is confirmed.

### 6.10 Observability preservation (critical ordering)

- **Init order:** the OTel SDK (`@opentelemetry/sdk-node`) and instrumentations must be **started before Fastify is imported/instantiated**, or the auto-instrumentation hooks won't attach. Keep this in a tracing bootstrap loaded first (e.g. `node -r ./dist/tracing.js` or the very first import in the entry file). Verify this didn't get reordered during the migration.
- Add `@opentelemetry/instrumentation-fastify` (it's part of `auto-instrumentations-node`, but confirm it's enabled) so Fastify routes produce spans equivalent to the old Express instrumentation.
- `elastic-apm-node` auto-instruments Fastify; confirm transactions are named by route and errors are captured.
- `prom-client`: keep the existing registry/metrics, or adopt `fastify-metrics` (which wraps `prom-client`) for HTTP metrics. Either way, **preserve metric names and labels** so dashboards/alerts keep working.

### 6.11 Raw body / webhook signature verification (gotcha)

If `raw-body` was used to verify webhook/HMAC signatures (common for payment/bank callbacks), Fastify parses JSON before your handler sees it, destroying the raw bytes. Use **`@fastify/raw-body`** (or a custom `addContentTypeParser` with `parseAs: 'buffer'`) on the specific signature-verifying routes to retain the raw buffer **alongside** the parsed body. Losing the raw body silently breaks signature checks — verify these routes explicitly.

### 6.12 Routing auto-load

Replace `require-all` + `routing-controllers` controller discovery with **`@fastify/autoload`** pointed at the routes/plugins directory. Preserve route prefixes (the old `routePrefix` / controller base paths) via autoload's `dirNameRoutePrefix` or explicit prefixes so URLs don't change.

### 6.13 Dual CJS + ESM preservation

- Keep the package's existing module system and build output. Fastify and all `@fastify/*` plugins are dual, so this is achievable.
- In **CJS** use `require('@fastify/x')` / `fastify.register(require('@fastify/x'))`; the `await fastify.register(import('@fastify/x'))` form in some docs is ESM-only — don't mix it into a CJS build.
- Before merging, **build and import the package both ways** (a `require()` smoke test and an `import` smoke test) to prove dual consumption still works. Do not add any ESM-only dependency without flagging it (§9).

